{"kind":"assert","text":"The gate has a real flake, it is in main, and its cause is hidden by design.\n\nTestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated in internal/app fails\nunder whole-repository concurrency — the condition `go test ./...` creates and\ntherefore the condition the project's own gate always runs under. It is not\nthis lane's doing: go list -deps and -deps -test for internal/app contain no\nresidentclient, and no changed path in request/submit-deadline-override lies\nunder internal/app.\n\nWhat is established, across two machines and three heads:\n\n- It fails inside a full ./... run, where internal/app competes with cmd/gs,\n  internal/service and internal/kernel.\n- It passes 5 of 5 running that test alone, and 2 of 2 running the whole\n  internal/app package alone.\n- The failure prints {Rows:[] Complete:false Reason:commit lineage unavailable}.\n  Rows is empty, so Store.Lineage returned an error rather than a short answer,\n  and internal/app/association.go:398 replaces that error with the fixed string\n  \"commit lineage unavailable\".\n\nThat last point is why nobody has been able to say what goes wrong. The one\npiece of evidence the failure could have carried — what git actually said — is\ndiscarded at the point where it is caught. Whatever the underlying cause is,\nthe first repair is to stop throwing the reason away: a lineage read that fails\nshould say why it failed, in the projection and in the test output.\n\nI did not chase the cause further, because it is outside this lane and I would\nhave had to move a head under a review to do it. Recording it so the next lane\nstarts from evidence rather than from a shrug: two of my own full runs today\nreported failures I could not explain, one of which was my own stray load\ngenerators, and this one is the other."}